# Web-app colours

Use these colours on web pages, Home Assistant dashboards and app screens. A dashboard uses the same civica theme in a browser and in the Home Assistant Companion app. Use a functional colour for its job first, such as the link or error colour.

<%= colour_copy_status %>
<% copy_hex = ->(hex, name) { %(<button type="button" class="c-copy-value" data-copy-value="#{hex}" aria-label="Copy hex #{hex} for #{name}" hidden>#{ColourHelpers::COPY_ICON}</button>) } %>

## Theme colour

Each household picks one theme colour with **Theme colour** in their Home Assistant profile. The 7 choices are the primary colours of the [Blues](#blues), [Greens](#greens), [Teals](#teals), [Purples](#purples), [Magentas](#magentas) and [Reds](#reds), and [Black](#neutrals). Blue is the default.

The theme colour fills the header, buttons, switches and highlights. Every other colour stays the same. [Theme colour](colour.html#theme-colour) lists what changes. [Change theme colours](../get-started/colour-settings.html) explains the control.

In a custom card, use the theme variable, not a hex value. In the Morgan household's Blue theme, `var(--civica-brand-primary)` is Primary blue. If they switch to Teal, the card turns Primary teal with no change.

## Web-app palette

Use these colours for supporting elements, such as an illustration or a custom card. Palette colours have no theme variable, so copy the value from the swatch. For text, links, borders, focus, errors and success, use the [functional colours](#functional-colours).

Most colours have tints at 25%, 50%, 80% and 95%, and shades at 25% and 50%. Blue also has a 10% shade. Black has tints for greys.

Some swatches have a note:

- **Web only**: the 4 browns
- **App only**: 7 bright accents and the 2 dark mode blues, for [app-like graphics](#app-colours)

Accent teal is for both.

The palette has:

<%= colour_group_links('web', 'functional colours' => '#functional-colours') %>

<%=
  web_groups = colour_page('web').fetch('palette')
  app_groups = colour_page('app').fetch('palette')
  web_ids = web_groups.flat_map { |group| group.fetch('colours') }
  app_ids = app_groups.flat_map { |group| group.fetch('colours') }
  titles = (web_groups + app_groups).map { |group| group.fetch('title') }.uniq
  titles.map do |title|
    ids = [web_groups, app_groups].map { |groups| groups.find { |group| group.fetch('title') == title }&.fetch('colours') || [] }.reduce(:|)
    swatches = ids.map do |id|
      note = if !app_ids.include?(id) then 'Web only' elsif !web_ids.include?(id) then 'App only' end
      colour_swatch(id, note: note)
    end
    %(<h3 id="#{colour_slug(title)}">#{escape(title)}</h3>\n) + colour_swatch_list(swatches)
  end.join("\n")
%>

## Functional colours

A functional colour has one job. Use it only for that job.

Use the civica theme variable, not the value, so the colour follows the theme colour and dark mode. In a custom card's CSS, write `var(--civica-border-quiet)`.

| Functional colour | Use it for | civica theme variable | In dark mode |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `brand` | The header | `civica-brand-primary`, which is the household's theme colour | The same colour |
| `brand` | Buttons, highlights and on states | `primary-color` | A lighter tint of the theme colour |
| `text` | Text | `primary-text-color` | White |
| `secondary-text` | Hints and less important text | `secondary-text-color` | `#CBD0D3`<%= copy_hex.('#CBD0D3', 'secondary-text in dark mode') %> |
| `inverse-text` | Text on buttons filled with the theme colour | `text-primary-color` | Black, because buttons use a light tint in dark mode |
| `link` | Links | `link-color` | Follows the theme colour |
| `border` | Dividers and quiet card edges | `civica-border-quiet` | `#50565A`<%= copy_hex.('#50565A', 'border in dark mode') %> |
| `input-border` | Fields people type in | `civica-input-border` | White |
| `focus` and `focus-text` | The element with keyboard focus, and its text | `civica-focus-background` and `civica-focus-text` | The same colours |
| `error` | Error messages only | `error-color` | `#F0C6C6`<%= copy_hex.('#F0C6C6', 'error in dark mode') %> |
| `success` | Success messages only | `success-color` | `#BCDACF`<%= copy_hex.('#BCDACF', 'success in dark mode') %> |

civica has no variable for `template-background`, `body-background`, `link-hover`, `link-visited`, `link-active`, `hover` or the surface colours. Home Assistant draws the page with `primary-background-color` and cards with `card-background-color`. civica sets both to White in light mode, and `#17191B`<%= copy_hex.('#17191B', 'the page background in dark mode') %> and `#24282B`<%= copy_hex.('#24282B', 'the card background in dark mode') %> in dark mode.

